WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.685
[Rue]
A lot of people ask
what I've been up to

2
00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:20.061
since high school.

3
00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:24.107
And honestly,
nothing good.

4
00:00:25.942 --> 00:00:28.111
[people shouting in Spanish]

5
00:00:29.946 --> 00:00:32.615
[shouting in Spanish continues]

6
00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:34.617
[engine revving]

7
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.580
Más fuerte.

8
00:00:38.621 --> 00:00:41.166
[engine revving]

9
00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.418
[shouting in Spanish continues]

10
00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.964
Gracias, mi amigos!
[laughing]

11
00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:51.217
-[engine revving]
-[all cheering]

12
00:00:51.259 --> 00:00:53.720
[♪ "Ride Like the Wind"
by Christopher Cross playing]

13
00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:56.139
-[volume increasing]
-Whoo!

14
00:00:56.181 --> 00:01:00.268
♪ It is the night,
my body's weak ♪

15
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:03.938
♪ I'm on the run,
no time to sleep ♪

16
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.609
-♪ I've got to ride,
ride like the wind ♪
-[Rue laughing]

17
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.361
[singing along]
♪ To be free again ♪

18
00:01:11.654 --> 00:01:14.949
♪ And I've got such
a long way to go ♪

19
00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:16.576
♪ Such a long way to go ♪

20
00:01:16.618 --> 00:01:19.496
♪ To make it to the border
of Mexico ♪

21
00:01:19.496 --> 00:01:23.208
♪ So I'll ride like the wind ♪

22
00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.667
♪ Ride like the wind ♪

23
00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:28.254
♪ Gonna ride like the wind ♪

24
00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:30.548
Whoo! [laughing]

25
00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:34.177
[singing melody]

26
00:01:35.011 --> 00:01:37.180
-Whoo!
-[music stops abruptly]

27
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.641
[metal creaking, clanging]

28
00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:43.478
[wind whooshing]

29
00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.484
[motorcycle revving]

30
00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:52.987
[♪ "Ride Like the Wind"
continues playing faintly]

31
00:01:53.029 --> 00:01:54.656
[exhales sharply]
Andale.

32
00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.702
[metal clunking]

33
00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:05.458
Fuck.

34
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.211
-[metal clanging]
-Whoa! Fuck.

35
00:02:14.551 --> 00:02:16.553
[metal scraping]

36
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.391
[creaking]

37
00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:25.061
Whoa.

38
00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:27.397
♪ To get to the border
of Mexico ♪

39
00:02:27.397 --> 00:02:30.900
♪ So I'll ride like the wind ♪

40
00:02:30.942 --> 00:02:32.277
Fuck!

41
00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:34.529
-[music turning off]
-Okay.

42
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.157
Shit.

43
00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:41.536
Go back.

44
00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:44.080
[engine revving]

45
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.373
Alright.

46
00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:48.585
-[grunting]
-[engine revving]

47
00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:50.378
[tires scraping]

48
00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.462
Come on.

49
00:02:52.922 --> 00:02:54.924
[engine revving]

50
00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:02.640
-[banging]
-[muttering indistinctly]

51
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.894
[turns off engine]

52
00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:07.437
[Rue sighs]

53
00:03:17.614 --> 00:03:18.823
Oh!

54
00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:20.450
[glass breaking]

55
00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:23.494
Ah. Fuck!

56
00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:28.958
[grunting]

57
00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:31.211
Put all my weight...

58
00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.715
-[grunting]
-[horn honks]

59
00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:36.758
Ah!

60
00:03:42.805 --> 00:03:44.641
[softly] Oh, shit.

61
00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.103
[metal creaking]

62
00:03:49.145 --> 00:03:51.314
-[wind whooshing]
-[Rue breathing heavily]

63
00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.994
[gasping, exclaiming]

64
00:04:04.994 --> 00:04:07.205
Ah! Ah! Ah!

65
00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:11.584
[♪ tense music playing]

66
00:04:18.174 --> 00:04:19.342
[exhales sharply]

67
00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:23.805
[metal creaking]

68
00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:27.809
Oh, shit. Ah, ah!

69
00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.314
Oh! Oh! Oh!

70
00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.948
♪♪

71
00:04:46.911 --> 00:04:48.538
There we go.

72
00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:58.256
Okay. Whew.

73
00:04:59.048 --> 00:05:01.259
[wind whistling]

74
00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:06.556
[♪ dramatic music playing]

75
00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.311
[hawk screeching]

76
00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:20.528
[whip cracks]

77
00:05:35.918 --> 00:05:39.339
[♪ dramatic music
continues playing]

78
00:05:59.942 --> 00:06:01.944
[rooster crowing]

79
00:06:01.944 --> 00:06:03.946
-[pig grunting]
-[cow mooing]

80
00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:07.450
[horse nickering]

81
00:06:26.177 --> 00:06:27.970
You want a cup of milk?

82
00:06:35.978 --> 00:06:37.647
[yawning] Sure.

83
00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:46.656
Thank you.

84
00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.541
That's the best fucking milk
I've ever had.

85
00:06:57.333 --> 00:06:58.709
It's Dahlia's.

86
00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:04.006
[Rue] Thank you, Dahlia.

87
00:07:04.006 --> 00:07:06.509
[Dahlia mooing]

88
00:07:06.551 --> 00:07:08.636
[Mr. Miller]
We wanna thank you,
Lord Jesus Christ,

89
00:07:08.678 --> 00:07:12.432
for giving us our daily bread
and forgiving us our trespasses.

90
00:07:13.808 --> 00:07:16.310
And Lord, please guide
our new friend Ruby

91
00:07:16.352 --> 00:07:18.563
and her college newspaper
to help expose

92
00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:21.983
the-- the pure evil that's
pouring across our border

93
00:07:22.024 --> 00:07:25.862
and poisoning our great nation,
the United States of America.

94
00:07:25.862 --> 00:07:27.113
Amen.

95
00:07:30.158 --> 00:07:31.242
Amen.

96
00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:33.786
-[all] Amen.
-[Mr. Miller] Let's eat.

97
00:07:33.828 --> 00:07:36.372
-Alright, pass the eggs.
-We're gonna help
with your plate, sweetie.

98
00:07:36.414 --> 00:07:39.125
You know who
gets it first, Dad.

99
00:07:39.167 --> 00:07:40.626
[Mr. Miller]
This is our address.

100
00:07:40.668 --> 00:07:43.296
Will you be sure to send your
article when it's in the paper?

101
00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:46.424
Yeah, as long as the commies
at college don't censor it.
[chuckles]

102
00:07:47.633 --> 00:07:50.761
-You're doing the Lord's work.
-Thank you.

103
00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.263
-Hope to see you again soon.
-Thank you so much.

104
00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:54.098
-Good luck with everything.
-Yeah.

105
00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.393
-Bye, guys.
-[all] Bye.

106
00:07:57.435 --> 00:07:59.645
-Wish you well.
-Thank you.

107
00:08:00.938 --> 00:08:02.064
-Aw.
-Safe travels.

108
00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:03.399
-Thank you.
-God bless!

109
00:08:03.399 --> 00:08:04.484
Bye.

110
00:08:04.525 --> 00:08:06.736
[♪ whimsical vocalizing]

111
00:08:12.408 --> 00:08:14.160
I just got my permit.

112
00:08:15.453 --> 00:08:17.663
[car starting]

113
00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:29.509
[Mr. Miller]
God bless, Ruby.

114
00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:32.887
[Daisy] I've never been
on the internet.

115
00:08:34.472 --> 00:08:37.350
[Rue] You know, Daisy,
I think you got it made.

116
00:08:37.391 --> 00:08:39.769
[♪ "Love is Like Oxygen"
by Sweet playing on car radio]

117
00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:41.771
But I can't help
but be curious.

118
00:08:43.731 --> 00:08:48.611
All the people out there
living big, exciting lives.

119
00:08:51.822 --> 00:08:53.908
I'd trade spots with you
in a heartbeat.

120
00:08:56.244 --> 00:08:57.245
Why?

121
00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:03.125
[radio volume increasing]

122
00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:09.465
♪ You get too much,
you get too high ♪

123
00:09:09.507 --> 00:09:12.510
♪ Not enough,
and you're gonna die ♪

124
00:09:18.641 --> 00:09:19.809
[Rue] Thank you.

125
00:09:23.145 --> 00:09:24.689
You ought to come back out
in the spring

126
00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.274
and meet Dahlia's baby.

127
00:09:28.484 --> 00:09:29.819
I just might.

128
00:09:41.706 --> 00:09:44.166
-Oh, shit, qué pasó!
-[laughing] USA.

129
00:09:44.166 --> 00:09:46.460
-Hey, USA, USA.
-USA.

130
00:09:46.502 --> 00:09:52.633
[group]
USA! USA! USA! USA! USA!

131
00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.931
[♪ hopeful music playing]

132
00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:19.869
[♪ hopeful music
continues playing]

133
00:10:36.677 --> 00:10:38.888
[rattlesnake rattling]

134
00:10:50.066 --> 00:10:52.068
[metal fan rattling]

135
00:10:53.569 --> 00:10:55.780
[wind whooshing]

136
00:11:14.757 --> 00:11:16.967
[TV playing faintly]

137
00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:18.803
[bird chirping]

138
00:11:22.473 --> 00:11:23.724
[bird calling]

139
00:11:23.766 --> 00:11:26.102
[bird babbling, whistling]

140
00:11:27.603 --> 00:11:29.647
[squawking, calling]

141
00:11:34.402 --> 00:11:36.278
[Laurie] Hello, Rue.

142
00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:38.864
-[bird chirps]
-Hey, baby.

143
00:11:38.906 --> 00:11:41.826
-Where's my car?
-[Rue] I had to leave it behind.

144
00:11:41.867 --> 00:11:45.121
[Laurie]
Hm, I guess I'll just
add it to your tab then.

145
00:11:46.288 --> 00:11:47.957
[Rue] A few years
after high school,

146
00:11:47.998 --> 00:11:49.834
I was working
at a smoke shop.

147
00:11:49.875 --> 00:11:51.877
[customer]
Could I just get
a raw cone, please?

148
00:11:51.919 --> 00:11:53.170
[Rue] Yeah, for sure.

149
00:11:56.132 --> 00:11:58.217
You can keep
the change actually, thanks.

150
00:11:59.468 --> 00:12:00.678
Okay.

151
00:12:11.272 --> 00:12:12.148
[chuckles] Laurie, hey.

152
00:12:12.189 --> 00:12:14.942
-How are you?
-You owe me money.

153
00:12:14.984 --> 00:12:17.361
No, I-- I know, yeah.
I was gonna pay you back.

154
00:12:17.403 --> 00:12:18.988
-You were?
-Yeah.

155
00:12:18.988 --> 00:12:22.158
Um, but then, you know,
you-- you moved.

156
00:12:22.199 --> 00:12:24.910
[chuckles] I don't really have,
like, ten grand right now.

157
00:12:24.952 --> 00:12:27.329
Oh, you don't owe me
ten grand, silly.

158
00:12:27.371 --> 00:12:28.998
-I don't?
-No.

159
00:12:28.998 --> 00:12:33.127
Um, you did 46 months ago,
and, um, now,

160
00:12:33.169 --> 00:12:37.173
$10,000 at a 20%
monthly interest rate

161
00:12:37.173 --> 00:12:42.011
over 46 months means that
you actually owe me, uh...

162
00:12:42.762 --> 00:12:46.849
$43,887,000.

163
00:12:48.476 --> 00:12:50.936
Uh, $43 million?

164
00:12:51.812 --> 00:12:54.273
[Laurie]
I'll settle for 100,000.

165
00:12:54.315 --> 00:12:57.193
But I bet you don't
have that either.

166
00:13:00.029 --> 00:13:03.365
[Rue] And that is how
I became a drug mule.

167
00:13:03.407 --> 00:13:05.159
[car horns honking]

168
00:13:05.201 --> 00:13:06.702
[dogs barking]

169
00:13:06.702 --> 00:13:09.914
[♪ dramatic Western music
playing]

170
00:13:17.588 --> 00:13:19.799
[people chattering]

171
00:13:27.807 --> 00:13:31.101
♪♪

172
00:13:45.407 --> 00:13:46.951
[Uno] Swallow.

173
00:13:46.992 --> 00:13:50.746
Um, I don't-- I don't really
think that's possible.

174
00:13:50.746 --> 00:13:52.414
[Uno chuckles]

175
00:13:52.414 --> 00:13:53.707
Claro que sí.

176
00:13:53.749 --> 00:13:55.417
Disfruta.

177
00:14:00.464 --> 00:14:02.091
[cigarette sizzling]

178
00:14:09.974 --> 00:14:12.226
[Rue]
The most important thing...

179
00:14:12.268 --> 00:14:14.812
is to make sure each balloon
is properly sealed.

180
00:14:20.192 --> 00:14:22.194
[grunting]

181
00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:25.948
[gulping]

182
00:14:25.948 --> 00:14:27.408
[gagging, coughing]

183
00:14:30.911 --> 00:14:32.121
[gagging]

184
00:14:33.163 --> 00:14:34.290
[gagging]

185
00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:38.460
After a few runs,
I decided to bring a friend.

186
00:14:42.006 --> 00:14:44.717
[gulping, retching]

187
00:14:44.758 --> 00:14:45.885
[Rue] Fuck.

188
00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:49.513
[gagging]

189
00:14:55.811 --> 00:14:58.022
[retching continues]

190
00:15:01.817 --> 00:15:04.028
[coughing] Ugh.

191
00:15:04.069 --> 00:15:05.654
The danger
of body packing is...

192
00:15:05.696 --> 00:15:06.989
[sighs]

193
00:15:07.031 --> 00:15:09.241
...if a balloon breaks,

194
00:15:09.283 --> 00:15:10.492
you die.

195
00:15:16.498 --> 00:15:17.958
[speaking Spanish]

196
00:15:30.012 --> 00:15:32.222
[indistinct chatter]

197
00:15:38.854 --> 00:15:40.856
[Rue] Faye, get your ass
in the car.

198
00:15:40.856 --> 00:15:42.733
Okay, we gotta cross by 3:00.

199
00:15:42.775 --> 00:15:45.986
[♪ dramatic music playing]

200
00:15:48.489 --> 00:15:51.200
[car horns honking]

201
00:15:59.583 --> 00:16:01.585
[police radio chatter]

202
00:16:07.883 --> 00:16:10.260
-[farting]
-Yo.

203
00:16:10.302 --> 00:16:11.428
Sorry.

204
00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:14.473
-Don't, man.
-I can only clench for so long.

205
00:16:14.515 --> 00:16:16.350
Yeah, I know,
but you gotta keep that shit

206
00:16:16.392 --> 00:16:18.477
up there, alright?
Breathe through it.

207
00:16:19.603 --> 00:16:21.355
-[farting]
-Damn!

208
00:16:21.397 --> 00:16:24.233
-Sorry.
-Faye, the fuck did you eat?

209
00:16:24.274 --> 00:16:26.402
-[speaking Spanish]
-I'm good, thank you.

210
00:16:26.402 --> 00:16:27.903
No, gracias.

211
00:16:27.903 --> 00:16:29.738
[dog barking]

212
00:16:33.242 --> 00:16:34.702
Turn the engine off, please.

213
00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:37.663
-[engine stopping]
-Passports.

214
00:16:43.836 --> 00:16:45.504
What were you ladies
doing in Mexico?

215
00:16:45.546 --> 00:16:47.965
Uh... [chuckles]
I was just showing my girl

216
00:16:48.007 --> 00:16:50.092
how the mamacitas
do it down here. [chuckles]

217
00:16:54.888 --> 00:16:57.141
-Ruby Bennett?
-That's me.

218
00:16:58.392 --> 00:16:59.560
Faye Valentine?

219
00:17:01.437 --> 00:17:02.438
You have fun?

220
00:17:03.439 --> 00:17:04.523
No.

221
00:17:06.483 --> 00:17:08.152
[Ruby chuckles]

222
00:17:13.866 --> 00:17:16.076
[dog barking]

223
00:17:19.121 --> 00:17:21.123
[dog sniffing]

224
00:17:21.123 --> 00:17:24.334
[♪ tense music playing]

225
00:17:29.465 --> 00:17:31.425
[dog sniffing]

226
00:17:37.514 --> 00:17:39.516
♪♪

227
00:17:51.195 --> 00:17:53.489
-Welcome to America.
-Thank you, sir.

228
00:17:55.032 --> 00:17:56.492
[car starting]

229
00:18:01.497 --> 00:18:03.082
-[birds squawking]
-[loud farting]

230
00:18:03.123 --> 00:18:05.167
Laurie! Harley!
She's about ready to blow.

231
00:18:05.167 --> 00:18:08.629
-[Rue] [straining] Oh yeah.
-Get the strainer.
It's in the dishwasher.

232
00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:10.172
-[Rue panting]
-My bad.

233
00:18:10.214 --> 00:18:12.174
-Come on, bro.
-[farting continues]

234
00:18:12.216 --> 00:18:14.301
-[Rue] Oh, shit.
-Hang in there.

235
00:18:14.343 --> 00:18:15.469
-Okay.
-Hang in there.

236
00:18:15.511 --> 00:18:17.262
-Okay. Okay.
-Okay, I got you.

237
00:18:17.304 --> 00:18:18.388
-[Rue groaning]
-The fuck are you doing?

238
00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:20.599
-Just breathe.
-I had an accident.

239
00:18:21.517 --> 00:18:24.019
-You better get the bucket.
-[Rue] Okay.

240
00:18:24.061 --> 00:18:25.646
[TV playing faintly]

241
00:18:26.522 --> 00:18:27.940
[bucket clanging]

242
00:18:27.981 --> 00:18:29.316
[Rue] And that,
ladies and gentlemen...

243
00:18:29.358 --> 00:18:31.276
-Damn!
-...is how fentanyl

244
00:18:31.318 --> 00:18:33.403
is smuggled
into the United States.

245
00:18:37.574 --> 00:18:40.077
[indistinct chatter]

246
00:18:43.038 --> 00:18:45.374
After Fezco's house
got raided,

247
00:18:45.374 --> 00:18:47.292
Laurie left East Highland
and went into business

248
00:18:47.334 --> 00:18:49.878
with her cousin Harley
and his son Wayne.

249
00:18:49.878 --> 00:18:52.131
[indistinct chatter]

250
00:18:52.172 --> 00:18:53.841
I'm not saying
they're inbred...

251
00:18:53.882 --> 00:18:55.342
[babbling]

252
00:18:55.384 --> 00:18:57.803
-...but I have my suspicions.
-Appreciate it.

253
00:18:57.845 --> 00:18:59.555
Looks good, Faye.

254
00:18:59.596 --> 00:19:00.889
[Harley] Here's to Rue.

255
00:19:00.889 --> 00:19:03.142
You haven't been killed
or caught yet.

256
00:19:03.183 --> 00:19:05.102
[chuckles]

257
00:19:05.144 --> 00:19:08.480
Praise the Lord.
[laughing]

258
00:19:08.522 --> 00:19:10.732
[Rue] I'd done a dozen runs
over the course of two years

259
00:19:10.774 --> 00:19:15.028
and somehow ended up
their number-one mule.

260
00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:17.406
[Wayne]
Guess you finally found
something you're good at.

261
00:19:18.907 --> 00:19:21.118
[rat squeaking]

262
00:19:24.121 --> 00:19:26.540
You think Wayne
liked the spaghetti?

263
00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:31.587
[Rue]
Mm, I think Wayne likes you.

264
00:19:31.628 --> 00:19:33.922
[chuckles softly]

265
00:19:33.922 --> 00:19:38.051
You know, he's trying to get
out of the drug business.

266
00:19:38.093 --> 00:19:39.887
[Rue] Yeah,
I don't know about that.

267
00:19:42.848 --> 00:19:44.266
He's been saving up.

268
00:19:44.308 --> 00:19:46.226
That's why he's got
a lot of money in his safe.

269
00:19:49.479 --> 00:19:50.898
What safe?

270
00:19:52.107 --> 00:19:53.442
[Faye]
The big one in his basement.

271
00:19:53.483 --> 00:19:55.235
He wants to get
into avocados,

272
00:19:55.277 --> 00:19:59.281
and you can make
a lot of money with avocados.

273
00:20:07.748 --> 00:20:09.625
What else does he have
in that safe?

274
00:20:09.666 --> 00:20:13.295
Uh, I don't know.
Important stuff.

275
00:20:14.171 --> 00:20:16.715
-Where's the key?
-On his belt.

276
00:20:18.425 --> 00:20:21.678
What if there was a way to,
like, take off his belt?

277
00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.722
[both laughing]

278
00:20:26.099 --> 00:20:27.643
I think I can
figure that out.

279
00:20:27.684 --> 00:20:28.852
[rooster crowing]

280
00:20:28.894 --> 00:20:31.396
[Rue] In between runs,
I'd pick up extra cash

281
00:20:31.438 --> 00:20:32.814
driving for Uber.

282
00:20:34.066 --> 00:20:35.400
[engine starting]

283
00:20:35.442 --> 00:20:39.279
[♪ "Sunset and Vine"
by Waylon Jennings playing]

284
00:20:39.321 --> 00:20:43.325
♪ I left the old farm
down in Texas ♪

285
00:20:43.367 --> 00:20:47.537
♪ And bummed
all around for a time ♪

286
00:20:47.579 --> 00:20:51.792
♪ Then one day
I found myself staring ♪

287
00:20:51.833 --> 00:20:56.088
♪ At a sign that read
Sunset and Vine ♪

288
00:20:56.129 --> 00:20:58.173
♪ The big cars
and the view... ♪

289
00:20:58.173 --> 00:20:59.716
[passenger]
This city is crumbling.

290
00:20:59.758 --> 00:21:03.595
Crime is out of control.
Somebody's got to do something.

291
00:21:03.637 --> 00:21:04.805
Yeah, totally.

292
00:21:04.846 --> 00:21:07.599
♪ I decided to be
a great actor ♪

293
00:21:07.641 --> 00:21:09.851
-Ooh, up here on the right.
-Oh, okay.

294
00:21:09.851 --> 00:21:12.229
♪ On the corner
of Sunset and Vine ♪

295
00:21:12.271 --> 00:21:14.314
Alright. Here you go.

296
00:21:14.356 --> 00:21:16.650
-Stay out of trouble.
-Yeah, will do, Batman.

297
00:21:16.692 --> 00:21:17.859
-[horns honking]
-[Batman sighs]

298
00:21:17.901 --> 00:21:19.528
I don't know,
why are we still auditioning?

299
00:21:19.569 --> 00:21:22.489
Boy Wonder.
Hey, babe, how are you?

300
00:21:28.537 --> 00:21:29.663
[knocking]

301
00:21:29.705 --> 00:21:31.873
[in low voice]
Yo, Howard!
It's Bennett, open up!

302
00:21:37.462 --> 00:21:39.589
-Hey!
-[chuckles] Hey.

303
00:21:39.631 --> 00:21:42.384
-How was Arizona?
-Um, yeah, no, it was great.

304
00:21:42.384 --> 00:21:43.969
Uh, just got
to hang out with Gia,

305
00:21:44.011 --> 00:21:45.262
and she's-- she's doing good.

306
00:21:45.304 --> 00:21:46.888
She's, like, top of her class.

307
00:21:46.888 --> 00:21:47.973
So was I.

308
00:21:48.974 --> 00:21:50.225
I know.

309
00:21:50.225 --> 00:21:52.185
[Rue]
Sometimes Lexi would
let me crash on her couch.

310
00:21:52.227 --> 00:21:54.604
[teapot whistling]

311
00:21:54.646 --> 00:21:56.648
But it always came
with a lecture.

312
00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:58.400
[Lexi] What are your
long-term goals?

313
00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:00.902
[Rue] And since
I couldn't be honest
about what I'd been up to...

314
00:22:00.944 --> 00:22:02.070
Uh...

315
00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:03.280
...I'd be vague.

316
00:22:03.322 --> 00:22:05.991
Right now, it's kinda
just a day at a time.

317
00:22:06.033 --> 00:22:08.410
You have to plan for the future,
because pretty soon

318
00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:10.746
Uber drivers
are gonna be extinct.

319
00:22:10.787 --> 00:22:12.789
It's all gonna be
automated, like AI.

320
00:22:12.831 --> 00:22:14.791
They're not gonna need
people like you.

321
00:22:16.543 --> 00:22:17.544
Right.

322
00:22:21.673 --> 00:22:24.468
You know, I met these,
uh, these Christians

323
00:22:24.509 --> 00:22:26.470
on a homestead in Texas.

324
00:22:26.511 --> 00:22:28.430
Christians in Texas?

325
00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:32.100
It was a drop-off.
But, yeah, the Miller family.

326
00:22:32.142 --> 00:22:34.436
I can't stop thinking
about them.

327
00:22:34.478 --> 00:22:38.440
There's like six kids,
and they lived on a farm

328
00:22:38.482 --> 00:22:40.275
with like a bunch of animals.

329
00:22:40.275 --> 00:22:42.944
And no technology.
No nothin'.

330
00:22:42.986 --> 00:22:46.365
And I genuinely think that
they were the happiest people

331
00:22:46.406 --> 00:22:47.616
I've ever met in my life.

332
00:22:47.657 --> 00:22:49.618
'Cause they're in a cult.

333
00:22:49.618 --> 00:22:51.536
Oh, well,
maybe that's the answer.

334
00:22:52.829 --> 00:22:54.122
Christianity?

335
00:22:54.122 --> 00:22:57.084
I just feel like
if I grew up religious,

336
00:22:57.125 --> 00:22:58.627
maybe my life
would be better.

337
00:22:58.627 --> 00:23:01.213
Well, I'm not gonna be
friends with a Christian.

338
00:23:01.254 --> 00:23:02.464
[scoffs] Why?

339
00:23:03.465 --> 00:23:05.550
'Cause they're judgmental.

340
00:23:12.224 --> 00:23:13.934
You know,
you really should call Fez.

341
00:23:16.019 --> 00:23:17.479
Yeah, I know.

342
00:23:18.522 --> 00:23:20.732
I feel guilty,
but I just-- I haven't--

343
00:23:20.774 --> 00:23:22.692
I haven't had any time,
I've been really busy.

344
00:23:22.734 --> 00:23:24.945
Yeah, well,
you're free today.

345
00:23:24.986 --> 00:23:28.615
This is rare. Like, I-- I work,
like, seven days a week.

346
00:23:29.574 --> 00:23:31.326
Yeah, well, he misses you.

347
00:23:31.326 --> 00:23:32.994
Did he say that?

348
00:23:32.994 --> 00:23:36.331
-Multiple times.
-Mm.

349
00:23:36.331 --> 00:23:39.543
I don't know,
my hours and his hours

350
00:23:39.584 --> 00:23:41.837
don't really line up,
so it's hard.

351
00:23:41.878 --> 00:23:44.381
No, just like, pick up
the phone and call him.

352
00:23:44.423 --> 00:23:46.133
It's not like
he's going anywhere.

353
00:23:47.134 --> 00:23:48.718
He's in prison for 30 years.

354
00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:53.098
[Rue] Lexi was
working in Hollywood

355
00:23:53.140 --> 00:23:54.683
on a nighttime soap.

356
00:23:57.894 --> 00:24:01.940
Her boss, Patty Lance,
was an industry legend.

357
00:24:01.982 --> 00:24:06.027
[Patty]
Writers are the engineers
of the human soul.

358
00:24:06.027 --> 00:24:10.365
People underestimate
the power of entertainment.

359
00:24:10.365 --> 00:24:12.617
What you see on television

360
00:24:12.659 --> 00:24:16.037
directly impacts the way
we see one another.

361
00:24:17.456 --> 00:24:21.042
The decisions we make
at home, in schools,

362
00:24:21.042 --> 00:24:25.547
and more importantly,
what we do at the ballot box.

363
00:24:25.547 --> 00:24:27.215
-[people snapping]
-Okay?

364
00:24:27.257 --> 00:24:31.887
That means it's really important
that we get 712 together.

365
00:24:31.928 --> 00:24:35.223
-[people snapping]
-[Rue] Lexi felt right at home.

366
00:24:35.223 --> 00:24:38.768
She was surrounded
by smart, creative people
who cared about the world.

367
00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:40.520
This election.

368
00:24:40.562 --> 00:24:43.440
It's giving me
so much anxiety.

369
00:24:43.482 --> 00:24:45.400
[Rue]
Unlike her sister,
who was living

370
00:24:45.442 --> 00:24:47.903
in some right-wing
suburban bubble.

371
00:24:47.944 --> 00:24:51.156
[♪ "(How Much is That)
Doggie in the Window"
by Patti Page playing]

372
00:24:52.908 --> 00:24:57.204
♪ How much is that doggie
in the window ♪

373
00:24:57.245 --> 00:24:58.830
[dog barking]

374
00:24:58.872 --> 00:25:04.419
♪ The one with
the waggly tail ♪

375
00:25:04.461 --> 00:25:05.754
[Rue]
She spent her free time

376
00:25:05.795 --> 00:25:07.422
trying to become
TikTok famous.

377
00:25:07.422 --> 00:25:09.090
-♪ In the window ♪
-[panting]

378
00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:13.094
-[dog barking]
-♪ I do hope that doggie ♪

379
00:25:13.094 --> 00:25:15.263
♪ Is for sale ♪

380
00:25:20.727 --> 00:25:23.772
♪ How much is that doggie ♪

381
00:25:23.813 --> 00:25:25.649
♪ In the window ♪

382
00:25:25.690 --> 00:25:28.235
[housekeeper] Very nice,
Miss Cassie, very nice.

383
00:25:29.528 --> 00:25:32.197
And wiggle, wiggle, wiggle.

384
00:25:32.239 --> 00:25:35.116
♪ How much is that doggie ♪

385
00:25:35.116 --> 00:25:37.410
-♪ In the window ♪
-[dog barking]

386
00:25:37.452 --> 00:25:39.162
[music stops abruptly]

387
00:25:40.497 --> 00:25:42.874
-[Nate] So, I go
to work all day.
-[toy squeaking]

388
00:25:42.916 --> 00:25:44.501
I come home,

389
00:25:44.543 --> 00:25:46.503
and you're pretending
to be a puppy dog.

390
00:25:46.545 --> 00:25:49.339
I'm just creating content.

391
00:25:49.381 --> 00:25:52.259
There-- there are
dishes in the sink
from last night's dinner.

392
00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:54.135
There are flies
buzzing around.

393
00:25:54.177 --> 00:25:55.512
I am so sorry, Mr. Nate.

394
00:25:55.554 --> 00:25:57.138
-Oh.
-[Cassie]
No, do not apologize, Juana.

395
00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.975
-You work for the two of us.
-She is a housekeeper,

396
00:26:00.016 --> 00:26:01.476
not a videographer.

397
00:26:01.518 --> 00:26:04.354
-Well, she's good at both.
-I pay her to do one.

398
00:26:04.396 --> 00:26:05.647
[snapping] Get to work.

399
00:26:05.647 --> 00:26:06.982
Yes, Mr. Nate.

400
00:26:11.903 --> 00:26:14.030
How many times do we have
to talk about this?

401
00:26:15.699 --> 00:26:17.784
I am in the real world
making deals.

402
00:26:17.826 --> 00:26:20.620
If an investor comes across
a video like this online,

403
00:26:20.662 --> 00:26:23.331
-I'm a joke.
-People make money doing this.

404
00:26:23.373 --> 00:26:24.624
[Nate] Think about
what you're saying.

405
00:26:24.666 --> 00:26:26.668
What you're actually saying.

406
00:26:26.710 --> 00:26:29.629
♪ How much is that
doggie in the window ♪

407
00:26:29.671 --> 00:26:31.798
-Who's the doggie?
-I'm the doggie.

408
00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:33.842
-Alright, so you're for sale?
-No.

409
00:26:33.883 --> 00:26:37.262
No, but what you're saying is,
"You can buy me."

410
00:26:37.304 --> 00:26:38.847
No, i-it's just a funny video.

411
00:26:38.888 --> 00:26:41.266
Except you're not a doggy.
You're a grown woman.

412
00:26:41.308 --> 00:26:42.934
So, that makes you a what?

413
00:26:47.564 --> 00:26:48.857
A prostitute.

414
00:26:49.858 --> 00:26:51.776
No, I am not a prostitute.

415
00:26:51.818 --> 00:26:53.695
Then stop acting like one.

416
00:26:53.737 --> 00:26:55.530
You are blowing this
way out of proportion.

417
00:26:55.572 --> 00:26:57.699
I am growing my following.

418
00:26:57.699 --> 00:27:00.869
This brand might sponsor me.
It's called monetization.

419
00:27:00.869 --> 00:27:01.870
Mm-hmm.

420
00:27:07.542 --> 00:27:09.711
Come. Tssh.

421
00:27:10.962 --> 00:27:12.380
[whistling]

422
00:27:14.132 --> 00:27:16.593
[bell softly jingling]

423
00:27:19.012 --> 00:27:20.597
Sit.

424
00:27:26.019 --> 00:27:28.438
You don't need to worry
about money.

425
00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:29.814
I've got money.

426
00:27:29.856 --> 00:27:33.068
Why haven't we remodeled
the living room?

427
00:27:33.109 --> 00:27:36.029
-Or the kitchen
or the master en suite?
-Ohh.

428
00:27:36.071 --> 00:27:37.572
Baby, we will.

429
00:27:37.572 --> 00:27:40.617
But right now,
I'm investing in myself.

430
00:27:40.659 --> 00:27:42.035
In the business.

431
00:27:42.827 --> 00:27:45.580
It takes money
to make money.

432
00:27:45.622 --> 00:27:47.290
-Then, let me chip in.
-No.

433
00:27:55.924 --> 00:27:58.760
You've been a bad, bad dog.

434
00:28:00.428 --> 00:28:01.763
Mm.

435
00:28:03.264 --> 00:28:04.933
Woof, woof.

436
00:28:06.434 --> 00:28:09.771
[Rue]
Nate had taken over
his dad's business.

437
00:28:09.813 --> 00:28:13.608
How 'bout this?
Just turn 'em on and pretend.

438
00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:16.319
-Pretend to do what?
-Work.

439
00:28:16.361 --> 00:28:18.655
[knocking]
We gotta put on a show.

440
00:28:20.448 --> 00:28:21.616
Alright, boss.

441
00:28:21.658 --> 00:28:23.618
[Rue]
But what he didn't realize

442
00:28:23.618 --> 00:28:25.495
is how fucking hard
it is to build

443
00:28:25.537 --> 00:28:27.122
in Southern California.

444
00:28:28.248 --> 00:28:30.291
Their wedding
was around the corner.

445
00:28:32.544 --> 00:28:34.713
And Cassie
was dreaming big.

446
00:28:35.505 --> 00:28:37.132
[Nate] Fuck.

447
00:28:46.558 --> 00:28:48.309
[sighs]

448
00:28:52.147 --> 00:28:54.149
[workers chattering]

449
00:29:03.032 --> 00:29:04.617
-How's it going, buddy?
-Nate, how are you?

450
00:29:04.659 --> 00:29:06.161
-Nice to see you.
-A lot going on.

451
00:29:06.161 --> 00:29:07.746
-Thank you for coming out.
-Holy cow!

452
00:29:07.787 --> 00:29:09.664
I know, it's busy.
It's all happening.

453
00:29:09.706 --> 00:29:11.082
I got an A-plus-plus team

454
00:29:11.124 --> 00:29:12.625
working around the clock
on this.

455
00:29:12.667 --> 00:29:14.085
You sure do.

456
00:29:17.922 --> 00:29:20.675
[Nate]
Do you know how many
baby boomers died today?

457
00:29:20.675 --> 00:29:21.801
No.

458
00:29:21.843 --> 00:29:24.512
-Six thousand.
-Jesus.

459
00:29:24.512 --> 00:29:26.181
A boomer dies
every 15 seconds.

460
00:29:26.181 --> 00:29:28.558
You fast-forward
ten years from now,

461
00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:30.602
it's a tidal wave of death.

462
00:29:30.643 --> 00:29:32.771
There's only two things
certain in this life,

463
00:29:32.812 --> 00:29:34.355
and that's death and taxes.

464
00:29:34.397 --> 00:29:38.443
Sun Settlers will be
the premier end-of-life
transitional facility

465
00:29:38.485 --> 00:29:40.069
in California.

466
00:29:40.111 --> 00:29:42.030
We're gonna offer
dignity and care

467
00:29:42.071 --> 00:29:43.740
when these people
need it most.

468
00:29:44.365 --> 00:29:45.784
You don't have to sell me.

469
00:29:45.825 --> 00:29:47.911
I just need to make sure
things are moving smoothly

470
00:29:47.952 --> 00:29:49.871
before I invest
in the second round.

471
00:29:49.871 --> 00:29:51.289
-Smoothly?
-Yeah.

472
00:29:52.248 --> 00:29:55.001
Take a look at what
we got going on here.

473
00:29:55.043 --> 00:29:57.212
These guys have been here
since the crack of dawn.

474
00:29:58.797 --> 00:30:00.882
Trust me, Kurt,
we're gonna make a killing.

475
00:30:00.882 --> 00:30:02.342
[Heather]
If Fred ever tried to tell me

476
00:30:02.383 --> 00:30:04.761
what I could
and could not post
on social media,

477
00:30:04.803 --> 00:30:07.138
I'd castrate him.

478
00:30:07.180 --> 00:30:09.390
He just doesn't get
the online world.

479
00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:11.476
I mean, people make
so much money using it.

480
00:30:11.518 --> 00:30:13.520
I don't want to have to fight
about the prices

481
00:30:13.561 --> 00:30:16.898
of floral arrangements,
or a wedding cake, or ice luges.

482
00:30:16.898 --> 00:30:18.691
I don't think you can
make money that fast.

483
00:30:18.733 --> 00:30:21.236
I mean,
you can on OnlyFans.

484
00:30:21.236 --> 00:30:22.695
Isn't that porn?

485
00:30:22.737 --> 00:30:24.447
See, I used to think
the same thing,

486
00:30:24.489 --> 00:30:26.115
but I started reading,

487
00:30:26.157 --> 00:30:28.284
and it's actually
the biggest misconception.

488
00:30:28.326 --> 00:30:31.621
I mean, the platform is used
to sell content, communicate.

489
00:30:31.663 --> 00:30:33.581
-I'd be the one in charge.
-Wait.

490
00:30:33.623 --> 00:30:35.375
Are you really
considering this?

491
00:30:35.416 --> 00:30:39.587
I mean, I wasn't, not until Nate
started nickel-and-diming me.

492
00:30:39.587 --> 00:30:42.257
So, what would you be posting?

493
00:30:42.298 --> 00:30:45.593
I don't know,
probably the same stuff
that I post on Instagram.

494
00:30:47.011 --> 00:30:49.305
So, why would people pay for it?

495
00:30:49.347 --> 00:30:51.474
To form a deeper
connection with me.

496
00:30:51.516 --> 00:30:55.270
Like, imagine all the unhappy
men in the world.

497
00:30:56.187 --> 00:30:57.522
For just $50,

498
00:30:57.564 --> 00:31:00.775
they could get a personalized
pic and text from me

499
00:31:00.817 --> 00:31:02.944
saying, "Good morning, handsome.

500
00:31:02.986 --> 00:31:04.863
Thinking about you today."

501
00:31:06.447 --> 00:31:07.574
That's it?

502
00:31:07.615 --> 00:31:09.576
People just want
to feel special.

503
00:31:14.956 --> 00:31:16.583
Well, good luck with Nate.

504
00:31:19.586 --> 00:31:21.212
Don't underestimate me.

505
00:31:22.005 --> 00:31:25.216
[♪ upbeat music playing]

506
00:31:37.812 --> 00:31:39.939
-Hey, Gillie.
-Hey.

507
00:31:39.981 --> 00:31:43.151
[♪ upbeat music
continues playing]

